 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the board change my unit's percentage to raise my share?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Your unit's percentage is set in the condo's founding papers. It cannot be changed without every owner's agreement, except as a phased condo's founding papers provide or when owners vote not to rebuild a unit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much say does one owner have in a vote on a special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As much as the unit's percentage. Where owners vote, a majority means owners holding at least 51 percent of the building's value, not 51 percent of the units."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a condo board borrow money instead of charging a special assessment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if the association is a nonprofit corporation and its articles do not forbid it. Under South Carolina law, it may borrow money and pledge its income to repay the loan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a special assessment matter if I pay cash?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. You owe your share like every other owner. Fannie Mae's loan rules do not stop a cash purchase, but they apply to a later buyer of your unit who needs a loan."}}]}</script>
