-<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/hoa/">HOA Guide</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Special Assessments</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">HOA Guide</p><h1 class="detail-h1">The buyer of a Myrtle Beach condo can owe the seller's special assessment<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">What one costs, who pays it, and how we find it in the minutes.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 1, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">An agent reads a Myrtle Beach building's minutes for you, so you know about a coming special assessment before you make an offer.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A special assessment is a one-time bill from a condo association, on top of the regular dues. It pays for a cost the budget and the reserve fund do not. South Carolina law puts no cap on one. Your share is your unit's percentage, set in the condo's founding papers, recorded when the condo was created.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If a seller leaves an assessment unpaid, the association can collect it from the buyer too. The buyer's limit is the amount on the association's written statement of what the seller owes. We get that statement before closing.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(